1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,088
Light travels along a silicon wire, a waveguide,

2
00:00:04,128 --> 00:00:06,537
and we want to split it evenly into two.

3
00:00:06,607 --> 00:00:08,463
A person would draw a smooth fork.

4
00:00:08,503 --> 00:00:13,500
Squeezed into this small box, it delivers only 86% of the light.

5
00:00:13,570 --> 00:00:16,126
A computer drew this instead: a strange,

6
00:00:16,166 --> 00:00:21,347
organic pattern in the same box. It delivers 99.8%.

7
00:00:21,417 --> 00:00:24,404
How does a computer invent shapes no human would draw?

8
00:00:24,474 --> 00:00:28,084
The trick is to run light backward. It's called the adjoint method.

9
00:00:28,904 --> 00:00:33,964
First, turn design into numbers: each pixel of the box gets a value p,

10
00:00:34,004 --> 00:00:36,496
from 0 for glass to 1 for silicon.

11
00:00:36,566 --> 00:00:39,232
A merit, F(p), scores the device:

12
00:00:39,272 --> 00:00:42,569
the fraction of light that reaches the two outputs.

13
00:00:42,639 --> 00:00:46,076
Picture F as a landscape over all possible designs.

14
00:00:46,116 --> 00:00:49,771
To improve, walk uphill, along the gradient.

15
00:00:49,841 --> 00:00:52,830
The obvious way to find it: nudge one pixel,

16
00:00:52,870 --> 00:00:56,733
rerun the whole simulation, and see how F changes.

17
00:00:56,803 --> 00:00:58,299
That's one simulation per pixel:

18
00:00:58,339 --> 00:01:02,645
four thousand for this little box, and millions for a metalens.

19
00:01:02,715 --> 00:01:05,294
With a million pixels and a minute per simulation,

20
00:01:05,334 --> 00:01:07,765
one step uphill would take two years.

21
00:01:08,585 --> 00:01:11,320
Here's the way out. At one frequency,

22
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:16,457
a wave simulation on a grid is one big linear system: A x = b.

23
00:01:16,527 --> 00:01:19,477
b is the source, x is the field everywhere,

24
00:01:19,517 --> 00:01:23,150
and the matrix A holds the material, so it depends on p.

25
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:26,683
Differentiate both sides with respect to one pixel,

26
00:01:26,723 --> 00:01:28,694
and solve for how the field changes.

27
00:01:28,764 --> 00:01:31,184
The merit's slope becomes a chain of three factors:

28
00:01:31,224 --> 00:01:35,135
a row, the inverse of A, and one column per pixel.

29
00:01:35,205 --> 00:01:39,089
Multiply right to left, and every column costs a solve with A:

30
00:01:39,129 --> 00:01:41,661
one simulation per pixel, again.

31
00:01:42,531 --> 00:01:47,108
But multiply left to right! The row times A⁻¹ is a single vector,

32
00:01:47,148 --> 00:01:50,043
λ, and it takes just one extra solve.

33
00:01:50,113 --> 00:01:52,525
Then each pixel's slope is a cheap product:

34
00:01:52,565 --> 00:01:57,005
two simulations give the whole gradient, however many pixels there are.

35
00:01:57,825 --> 00:02:01,151
So what is λ, physically? Light obeys reciprocity:

36
00:02:01,191 --> 00:02:05,381
swap a source and a detector, and the signal stays the same.

37
00:02:05,451 --> 00:02:10,106
In matrix terms, Aᵀ = A. So λ comes from the same simulation,

38
00:02:10,145 --> 00:02:14,581
with the source moved to the outputs: light sent backward.

39
00:02:14,651 --> 00:02:17,798
Nudge one pixel, and it scatters a tiny wavelet,

40
00:02:17,838 --> 00:02:20,528
with a strength set by the forward light hitting it.

41
00:02:20,598 --> 00:02:23,967
The backward field there tells how that wavelet reaches the outputs,

42
00:02:24,007 --> 00:02:27,528
and whether it arrives in step with the light already there.

43
00:02:27,598 --> 00:02:31,358
So each pixel's slope is the forward field times the adjoint field:

44
00:02:31,398 --> 00:02:34,859
the sensitivity map, for every pixel at once.

45
00:02:34,929 --> 00:02:36,949
Update every pixel, simulate again,

46
00:02:36,989 --> 00:02:39,944
and repeat. Watch the device take shape.

47
00:02:40,814 --> 00:02:43,965
Light run backward tells every pixel what to do.

48
00:02:44,785 --> 00:02:47,288
You don't even need to derive adjoints by hand.

49
00:02:47,328 --> 00:02:51,538
Take a phase mask, then episode one's free-space propagation.

50
00:02:51,608 --> 00:02:54,531
It's a chain: multiply by the mask, FFT,

51
00:02:54,571 --> 00:02:59,495
multiply by the transfer function H, inverse FFT, then square.

52
00:02:59,565 --> 00:03:02,401
Automatic differentiation, the engine of deep learning,

53
00:03:02,441 --> 00:03:07,310
runs the chain backward: FFTs become inverse FFTs,

54
00:03:07,350 --> 00:03:09,983
and H becomes its conjugate.

55
00:03:10,853 --> 00:03:14,878
For propagating waves, conjugating H just flips the sign of z.

56
00:03:14,918 --> 00:03:18,191
The backward pass really does send light backward.

57
00:03:18,261 --> 00:03:21,505
Now gradient descent shapes the mask to project any picture.

58
00:03:21,545 --> 00:03:24,916
Watch the image sharpen as the error falls.

59
00:03:24,986 --> 00:03:28,531
Episode five's Gerchberg–Saxton loop was a clever heuristic;

60
00:03:28,571 --> 00:03:32,310
gradients make it an optimization with any goal.

61
00:03:33,130 --> 00:03:36,858
Now go further: design the optics and the software together.

62
00:03:36,928 --> 00:03:40,128
A camera records a scene through our optics, adding noise;

63
00:03:40,168 --> 00:03:44,668
then an algorithm, here episode three's Wiener filter, sharpens it.

64
00:03:44,738 --> 00:03:46,256
Compare the result with the truth,

65
00:03:46,296 --> 00:03:48,364
and send the gradient back through everything:

66
00:03:48,404 --> 00:03:52,171
the filter, the noise, the light, into the optics.

67
00:03:52,241 --> 00:03:54,472
Ask for sharp images at every depth.

68
00:03:54,512 --> 00:03:57,224
An ordinary lens is sharp only in focus;

69
00:03:57,264 --> 00:04:01,718
elsewhere, its point-spread function, the PSF, blurs.

70
00:04:01,788 --> 00:04:04,066
The learned optics gives up a perfect focus

71
00:04:04,106 --> 00:04:07,378
for a PSF that hardly changes with depth,

72
00:04:07,418 --> 00:04:09,781
which the filter can undo everywhere.

73
00:04:10,601 --> 00:04:12,860
In code, it's a three-line loop: simulate,

74
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:15,936
take the gradient, step, a few hundred times.

75
00:04:16,006 --> 00:04:21,033
Real devices add rules: each pixel must be all silicon or all glass,

76
00:04:21,073 --> 00:04:23,673
with no feature too small to fabricate.

77
00:04:23,743 --> 00:04:26,694
The landscape has many hills, so different starting guesses

78
00:04:26,734 --> 00:04:30,887
climb to different designs: all good, none guaranteed best.

79
00:04:30,957 --> 00:04:33,696
And the optimizer exploits every error in its model:

80
00:04:33,736 --> 00:04:36,589
this lens shines in a cheap simulation,

81
00:04:36,629 --> 00:04:39,585
but loses half its light in a careful one.

82
00:04:39,655 --> 00:04:42,419
So inverse design needs models fast enough to run

83
00:04:42,459 --> 00:04:45,554
thousands of times, and accurate enough to trust.

84
00:04:46,374 --> 00:04:48,828
One forward and one backward simulation

85
00:04:48,868 --> 00:04:51,640
give the gradient for every pixel at once.

86
00:04:51,710 --> 00:04:54,369
And that completes our season: plane waves,

87
00:04:54,409 --> 00:04:57,412
lenses as Fourier transforms, the diffraction limit,

88
00:04:57,452 --> 00:05:00,898
coherence, phase, flat optics, periodic structures,

89
00:05:00,938 --> 00:05:04,203
trapped light, Maxwell on a grid, and design.

90
00:05:04,273 --> 00:05:05,963
Ten ideas, one thread.

91
00:05:06,633 --> 00:05:09,659
Light is linear: in the right basis, the physics is simple;

92
00:05:09,699 --> 00:05:12,486
run it backward, and the design is too.

